-- title:
--   (35) — under Assumption B and the eMFCQ at all limit points, $\|d(x^\nu)\|\to0$ along a bounded run
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $K\subseteq\mathbb R^n$ is nonempty, closed and convex, $f$ and $g=(g_1,\dots,g_m)$ are $C^{1,1}$ on an open set containing $K$, $\lambda\in(0,1)$ and $0<\rho<\beta$ are the parameters of $\kappa$, and the surrogates $\tilde f,\tilde g$ satisfy Assumption A with modulus $c>0$.
--
--   Let $d$ be the solution map of $(\mathrm P_x)$ on $K$, and let $(x^\nu)$ be a non-terminating run of Algorithm 1 with stepsizes $(\gamma^\nu)$ whose iterates are bounded. If Assumption B holds and the eMFCQ holds at every limit point of $(x^\nu)$, then
--   $$\lim_{\nu\to\infty}\|d(x^\nu)\|=0. \tag{35}$$
--   This upgrades $\liminf_\nu\|d(x^\nu)\|=0$, (34), to a full limit and yields part (ii) of Theorem 1.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: A1 and A5: strong convexity of f̃(•;x) and convexity of g̃_i(•;x) on the open set O_d → strong convexity and convexity on the box βBⁿ∞ ⊆ O_d; reason: O_d need not be convex; every use of A1 and A5 is at points of βBⁿ∞, so the hypothesis is weaker and the statement specializes to the source's
--   - encoding: d(x) = argmin of (Pₓ), the unique solution → a map d with hypothesis IsSubproblemSolutionMap (d(x) ∈ X̃(x) minimizes f̃(•;x) for every x ∈ K); reason: the minimizer exists and is unique under Assumption A (milestone subproblem_well_posed), so d is the source's map on K
--   - reading: the sequence {x^ν} generated by Algorithm 1 → an infinite run in which no iterate is generalized stationary (the test (S.1) never stops it); reason: the statement concerns limit points of an infinite sequence; a run that stops at (S.1) returns a generalized stationary point
--   - encoding: limit point of {x^ν} → MapClusterPt x̂ atTop x; reason: cluster point of the sequence; same notion in ℝⁿ
--   - encoding: classical normal cones N_K(x), N_{ℝᵐ₋}(y), empty off the set → published normalCone (defined everywhere) used only at points of the set, and negOrthantNormalCone with the feasibility clause built in; reason: same sets at every point where the source uses them
--   - generalization: g : ℝⁿ → ℝᵐ with constraint indices i ∈ {1,…,m}, at least one constraint (max_i over a nonempty index set) → any m : ℕ, including m = 0, with max_i{g_i(x)+} = 0 and every constraint clause empty; reason: at m ≥ 1 the statement is the source's; m = 0 only adds the problem without constraints
-- source:
--   Facchinei, Kungurtsev, Lampariello & Scutari, Ghost Penalties in Nonconvex Constrained Optimization, arXiv:1709.03384v3, Theorem 1, proof, (35), p.16

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_GhostPenalty_StandingAssumptions
import Definitions.Def_GhostPenalty_AssumptionA
import Definitions.Def_GhostPenalty_AssumptionB
import Definitions.Def_GhostPenalty_IsSubproblemSolutionMap
import Definitions.Def_GhostPenalty_IsDSMRun
import Definitions.Def_GhostPenalty_EMFCQ
open Filter Topology

namespace GhostPenalty

/-- **(35): under Assumption B the directions vanish along a bounded run.**
Facchinei, Kungurtsev, Lampariello & Scutari, *Ghost Penalties in Nonconvex Constrained
Optimization*, arXiv:1709.03384v3, Theorem 1, proof, (35), p.16: "Observe that if, instead of the weaker (34),
`lim_{ν→∞} ‖d(x^ν)‖ = 0` (35) holds, we can reason similarly … and conclude that (ii) holds.
Therefore, it is enough to show that Assumption B entails (35)." The setting is that of
Theorem 1 (ii) in the bounded case: "if, in addition, the eMFCQ holds at every limit point of
`{x^ν}`, under Assumption B".

For a non-terminating run of Algorithm 1 whose iterates are bounded, if Assumption B holds and
the eMFCQ holds at every limit point of the iterates, then `‖d(x^ν)‖ → 0`.

The standing hypotheses are the blanket assumptions on (P) (`StandingAssumptions`: `K`
nonempty closed convex, `f`, `g` C^{1,1} on an open set containing `K`), the parameters
`λ ∈ (0,1)` (`lam`), `ρ ∈ (0,β)` of (8), and Assumption A for the surrogates `f̃ = ft`,
`g̃ = gt` with neighbourhoods `O_d = Od`, `O_x = Ox` and modulus `c`. `d` solves (Pₓ) at every `x ∈ K`; the run is `IsDSMRun` (stepsizes in `(0,1]` with (3),
`x⁰ ∈ K`, no iterate generalized stationary, `x^{ν+1} = x^ν + γ^ν d(x^ν)`). Limit points are
cluster points of the sequence (`MapClusterPt`). -/
theorem eq_35 {n m : ℕ} (K : Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))) (f : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ)
    (g : Fin m → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ) (hP : StandingAssumptions K f g)
    (β lam ρ c : ℝ) (hlam : lam ∈ Set.Ioo (0 : ℝ) 1) (hρ : ρ ∈ Set.Ioo 0 β)
    (ft : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ)
    (gt : Fin m → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → ℝ)
    (Od Ox : Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))) (hA : AssumptionA f g K β ft gt Od Ox c)
    (d : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))
    (hd : IsSubproblemSolutionMap ft g gt K lam ρ β d)
    (x : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)) (γ : ℕ → ℝ) (hrun : IsDSMRun f g K d x γ)
    (hbdd : Bornology.IsBounded (Set.range x)) (hB : AssumptionB K d)
    (hcq : ∀ xh, MapClusterPt xh atTop x → EMFCQ g K xh) :
    Tendsto (fun ν => ‖d (x ν)‖) atTop (𝓝 0) := by sorry

end GhostPenalty
